\begin{framed}
The advent of \xintexprname 2.0, also known as \calxname, is almost entirely
backward compatible.  There are a few breaking changes and probably not
all of them are properly documented for the \dtt{2.0a1} release.  
\end{framed}

\subsection{Usage}

Loading:
\begin{everbatim}
\usepackage{calx}% with LaTeX
\input calx.sty\relax with other e-TeX formats.
\end{everbatim}

Example:
\begin{everbatim*}
$2^{200}=\calxinteval{2^200}$%  \calxinteval and \xintiieval are the same thing.

\noindent % \calxeval and \xintfloateval are the same thing.
$(\ln(3), \pi)\approx (\calxeval{ln(3), Pi})$

\xintDigits:= 32\relax % or \xintSetDigits{32}
\noindent % with 32 digits (hence, here, 31 decimal places).
$(\ln(3), \pi) \approx (\calxeval{ln(3), Pi})$

\xintDigits:= 16\relax % back to default setting
\end{everbatim*}

You can set the floating point precision up to \dtt{100}. For tips on
how to let \TeX{} print such long numeric output without overflowing
into the right margin, please search for |\printnumber| in the legacy
manual (which is actually the bulk of this PDF).

Note: loading \xintexprname automatically loads \calxname.  So
legacy documents automatically benefit from the speed-up and
higher accuracy.

\subsection{What is \calxname?}

It is the new arithmetic unit (floating point and big integers) of
\xintexprname at release 2.0. It is a complete rewrite of thousands of
lines of code, and addition of thousands more%
%
\footnote{Roughly the whole codebase has increased by 50\%. If one loads
  only \calxname, the "size" is increased only about 25\% compared with
  what applied with 1.4 series \xintexprname, due to \calxname not
  providing the support for fractions.},
%
which was done in October 2026 (and took the whole month to get
completed) by (mainly) Claude Opus 5.5, and ChatGPT 6. This was under
the training, guidance and general supervision by an anonymous human
assistant to Professor B..

NOTA BENE: \emph{the anonymous assistant checked only very little of the
  produced code and was each time horrified by what he saw.}  He tried
to fix a few obvious sub-optimalities and to get the AI assistants to
learn from him (which he thought they had, but no), with little success.
Much remains to be done at time of the \texttt{2.0a1} release for
cleaning up the code. But it works.

