The whole process was much more difficult and lengthy than originally
envisioned. It reached the limits of what the anonymous assistant was
willing to devote to an open source project with no financial
gratification (especially when on a few occasions the human did buy some
additional credits on top of the basic monthly subscription).  However
it was seen as an escape from the Apocalypse of July-August-September
2026 which completely changed the field of interest of the
anonymous assistant: mathematics.  Its sociology is going to
change for the worse very quickly and irremediably now.

Contrarily to \xintexprname, \calxname loads \emph{none} of \xintcorename,
\xintname, \xintfracname.  \textbf{It does not handle fractions}. For this you
must load \xintexprname package for its |\xinteval| evaluator.

For mathematical functions the target accuracy was set at a maximal
error of \texttt{0.005ulp}. This means roughly that correct rounding is
achieved in \dtt{99.5\%} of the cases (because at distance
\texttt{0.005ulp} from a tie, there is \dtt{50\%} chance to be wrongly
rounded).  It seems that trigonometry actually achieved ten times better
results, which may be modified in next relase to increase speed.  For
example for \dtt{4872} cases involvoing \func{log}, \func{exp},
\func{log1p}, \func{expm1}, only \dtt{21} were not correctly rounded and
the error was then \texttt{1ulp}.  Among \dtt{20300} trigonometrical
evaluations \dtt{19} were not correctly rounded, and \dtt{18} among them
were for \dtt{$P\leq8$}.

The \dtt{$P=8$} mode has an about ten times degraded target accuracy,
speed being the priority. (The core on arithmetic operations has not
yet been optimized for \dtt{$P=8$}, it probably shares some overhead
with the general precision case).


\subsection{Timing comparisons versus \cs{fpeval} for \func{sin},
  \func{ln}, \func{exp}, \dots}
\label{sec:calx-speed}


% Build-time comparison of \calxname 2.0a1 and LaTeX3 floating point evaluation.
% The first run writes \jobname-benchmark.tex; subsequent runs reuse it.
% Delete that file to remeasure after changing the code, engine or machine.

\def\CalxBenchmarkExprSin{sin(1.2345678)}
\def\CalxBenchmarkExprCos{cos(1.2345678)}
\def\CalxBenchmarkExprTan{tan(1.2345678)}
\def\CalxBenchmarkExprAsin{asin(0.56784321)}
\def\CalxBenchmarkExprAcos{acos(0.56784321)}
\def\CalxBenchmarkExprAtan{atan(1.2345678)}
\def\CalxBenchmarkExprLog{ln(2.3456789)}
\def\CalxBenchmarkExprExp{exp(1.2345678)}
\def\CalxBenchmarkExprPowI{12.3456^78.9012}
\def\CalxBenchmarkExprPowG{1.23456^789.012}
\newcommand\CalxBenchmarkLabel[1]{%
  \texttt{\detokenize\expandafter{#1}}}

% Only the l3benchmark interface needs expl3 syntax.
\ExplSyntaxOn
\fp_gset:Nn \g_benchmark_duration_target_fp { 0.025 }
\cs_new_protected:Npn \CalxBenchmarkMeasure #1
  {
    \benchmark_silent:n {#1}
    \xdef\CalxBenchmarkLastTime{\fp_to_decimal:N \g_benchmark_time_fp}
  }
\ExplSyntaxOff

% Benchmark raw timings are ordinary macros named
% \calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@<expression>@<precision> (accessed via \csname).
% The reference timing has the name calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@unit.
\def\CalxBenchmarkOne#1#2{%
  \CalxBenchmarkMeasure{\edef\calxDocumentBenchmarkSink{\fpeval{#2}}}%
  \expandafter\xdef\csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@#1@fp\endcsname
    {\CalxBenchmarkLastTime}%
  \xintFor ##1 in {8,16,32}\do{%
    \begingroup
      \xintDigits=##1\relax
      \CalxBenchmarkMeasure{\edef\calxDocumentBenchmarkSink{\calxeval{#2}}}%
      \expandafter\xdef\csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@#1@p##1\endcsname
        {\CalxBenchmarkLastTime}%
    \endgroup
  }%
}

\def\DOTIMINGS{%
  \def\CalxBenchmarkSum{0}%
  \xintFor ##1 in {1,2,3,4,5}\do{%
    \CalxBenchmarkMeasure{\edef\calxDocumentBenchmarkSink{\fpeval{sin(1)}}}%
    \edef\CalxBenchmarkSum
      {\xintfloateval{\CalxBenchmarkSum+\CalxBenchmarkLastTime}}%
  }%
  \expandafter\xdef\csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@unit\endcsname
    {\xintfloateval{\CalxBenchmarkSum/5}}%
  \xintForpair ##1##2 in
    {(sin,Sin),(cos,Cos),(tan,Tan),(asin,Asin),(acos,Acos),
     (atan,Atan),(log,Log),(exp,Exp),(powi,PowI),(powg,PowG)}\do{%
    \CalxBenchmarkOne{##1}{\csname CalxBenchmarkExpr##2\endcsname}%
  }%
}

% Cache the raw per-evaluation times, not the displayed rounded ratios.
\newwrite\CalxBenchmarkStream
\def\CalxBenchmarkWriteRaw#1{%
  \immediate\write\CalxBenchmarkStream{%
    \string\expandafter\string\gdef
    \string\csname\space calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@#1\string\endcsname
    {\csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@#1\endcsname}}%
}
\def\CalxBenchmarkSaveResults{%
  \immediate\openout\CalxBenchmarkStream=\jobname-benchmark.tex\relax
  \CalxBenchmarkWriteRaw{unit}%
  \xintFor ##1 in {sin,cos,tan,asin,acos,atan,log,exp,powi,powg}\do{%
    \xintFor ##2 in {fp,p8,p16,p32}\do{%
      \CalxBenchmarkWriteRaw{##1@##2}%
    }%
  }%
  \immediate\closeout\CalxBenchmarkStream
}

% All seven displayed columns are rounded with \xintieval[2].
\def\CalxBenchmarkPrepareTable{%
  \xintFor ##1 in {sin,cos,tan,asin,acos,atan,log,exp,powi,powg}\do{%
    \expandafter\xdef\csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRatio@##1@fp\endcsname{%
      \xintieval[2]{%
        \csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@##1@fp\endcsname/
        \csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@unit\endcsname}}%
    \xintFor ##2 in {p8,p16,p32}\do{%
      \expandafter\xdef\csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRatio@##1@##2units\endcsname{%
        \xintieval[2]{%
          \csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@##1@##2\endcsname/
          \csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@unit\endcsname}}%
      \expandafter\xdef\csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRatio@##1@##2\endcsname{%
        \xintieval[2]{%
          \csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@##1@##2\endcsname/
          \csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRaw@##1@fp\endcsname}}%
    }%
  }%
}
\def\CalxBenchmarkCell#1#2{\csname calxDocumentBenchmarkRatio@#1@#2\endcsname}

The \autoref{tab:timings} compares |\fpeval| with |\calxeval| used at precision
settings P=8, 16 and 32 (P is the value set by \csbxint{SetDigits}).  The
average evaluation time of |\fpeval{sin(1)}| is used as a time unit for
the entries in the |\fpeval| columns.
The |\calxeval| columns show the ratios of \calxname timings with those of
|\fpeval|. Thus, a value below one indicates that \calxname is faster, and a
value above one that it is slower.  All measurements include expansion of the
expression and assignment of its result to a macro via |\edef|.  In
particular they include the overhead of the respective parsers gathering
the input and printing the output.

