\subsection{User interface}

The user interface is reduced to:
\begin{itemize}
\item |\calxeval| or its legacy alias \csbxint{floateval}: a parser
      of floating point expressions at a given arbitrary precision P which
      can be any integer from 1 to 100 and is set by \csbxint{SetDigits}.
      For details of the available syntax elements see the legacy
      \xintexprname manual.
      Trigonometry and logarithm/exponential/powers are all
      available (the legacy maximal precision was 62).
\item |\calxinteval| or its legacy alias \csbxint{iieval}: handles
      arbitrarily large integers. Refer to the legacy \xintexprname
      manual.
    \item The interface to set the floating point precision via
      \csbxint{SetDigits}\marg{P} or equivalently
      \csbxint{Digits}|=|\meta{P}|\relax|. Their starred variants are
      now aliases to the non-starred ones.  Modifying the precision does
      not trigger a reload of the scientific libraries: they adjust
      automatically with no reloading needed.  The only overhead is the
      re-assignment of the trigonometrical constants documented in the
      legacy manual, such as \texttt{Pi}.  The legacy scientific
      libraries |xintlog.sty| and |xinttrig.sty| are gone, replaced by the
      \calxname ones.
\item The legacy \xintexprname interface to define functions and
  variables: \csbxint{NewFunction}, \csbxint{deffloatfunc}, \csbxint{deffloatvar},
  \csbxint{defiifunc}, \csbxint{defiivar}.
\item Things such as \csbxint{globaldefstrue}, \csbxint{verbosetrue},
      \csbxint{thecoords}, and a few others.
    \item |\calxsetup| which is described in the next section devoted to
      the handling of condition signals.
\end{itemize}

The packages \xintkernelname, \xintbinhexname, and \xinttoolsname are loaded automatically.

Loading \xintexprname does not modify anything to \calxname interface
and functionalities. It adds to it the \csbxint{eval} parser (together
with \csbxint{deffunc}, \csbxint{defvar}) and loads all of
\xintcorename, \xintname, and \xintfracname.  The macro interface of the
latter is unchanged but arithmetic internals are remapped to the
\calxname substratum.  Some undocumented at user level macros of
\xintfracname having to do with floating point numbers are gone.

The \texttt{2.0a1} release has a few breaking changes (behavior and
availability of \func{qfloat} and \func{qfrac} come to mind) which may
not have been documented in time.  The code comments are very likely
either outdated or barely legible.

\subsection{Arithmetic signals, exceptional results and error handling}
\label{sec:calx-signals}
\etocsetnexttocdepth{subsubsection}
\localtableofcontents

\textbf{Summary:}  \emph{an arithmetic condition is not necessarily a \TeX\ error.  In fact,
by default \calxname~\texttt{2.0a1} in case of division by zero returns a
non-finite result (\textbf{NaN}, \textbf{+Infinity}, or \textbf{-Infinity})
while the compilation continues: \emph{more generally, all arithmetic traps
  are disabled initially}.}

A \emph{trap} raises an error message and causes
\TeX{} to consider that a build error has arisen; applications which prefer
active traps may enable them individually one-by-one or globally.

The codebase was already done by Professor B. in 2017 but was left
then in a state where all error conditions are trapped, i.e.\@ do
trigger \TeX{} errors.

\begin{framed}
  The whole signal-handler system, described next, is available only for
  arithmetic with |\calxeval| (aka \csbxint{floateval}, i.e.\@ floating
  point calculations) and |\calxinteval| (aka \csbxint{iieval}, i.e.\@
  big integers calculations).  The legacy evaluator \csbxint{eval} (which
  handles fractions but also authorizes the mathematical functions) does not
  support the special values \texttt{NaN}, \texttt{+Infinity} and
  \texttt{-Infinity} as arithmetic operands.  Note that the fact that
  \csbxint{eval} authorizes mathematical functions such as \func{sin} and
  \func{exp} should not be abused: any multiplication of two such creates
  mantissas twice as big, obviously.
\end{framed}

\subsubsection{Default: division by zero without a \TeX\ error}

The following code does not interrupt the compilation:
\begin{everbatim*}
\calxresetflags
\edef\result{\calxeval{1/0}}
% \result expands to +Infinity
\calxifflagraised{DivisionByZero}
  {Division by zero occurred.}
  {No division by zero occurred.} The result is \result.
\end{everbatim*}

Similarly |\calxeval{-1/0}| produces \dtt{\calxeval{-1/0}}.

The calculation raises the \texttt{DivisionByZero} flag and returns
\texttt{+Infinity}.  Since \calxname has no signed zero, division of a nonzero
numerator by zero uses the numerator's sign to determine the infinity.  For
|\calxeval{0/0}|, the result is instead \texttt{NaN}; this is the
\texttt{DivisionUndefined} condition, which raises the
\texttt{InvalidOperation} flag rather than \texttt{DivisionByZero}.

A flag is raised when the expression is actually expanded, as in the
|\edef| above.  Merely storing an unevaluated expression via |\def|
or, in \LaTeX, |\newcommand|, does not raise a flag.

To make division by zero a \TeX\ error, enable its trap:
\begin{everbatim}
\calxsetup{DivisionByZero.trap=true}
\end{everbatim}
A trapped condition emits an expandable \TeX\ error and returns the
operation's trap value.  To restore the default behavior, use:
\begin{everbatim}
\calxsetup{DivisionByZero.trap=false}
\end{everbatim}

\subsubsection{Exceptional values and their printed forms}

The expression parsers |\calxeval| and |\calxinteval| recognize
\texttt{NaN} and \texttt{Infinity} as reserved constants.  A negative infinity
can be written \texttt{-Infinity}.  Thus, the following expressions all yield
a non-finite result, without necessarily raising a new signal:
\begin{everbatim*}
\calxeval{NaN, Infinity, -Infinity}
\end{everbatim*}

In particular, using a special constant is not the same as signalling
an arithmetic condition.

An application which prefers \texttt{NaN} for division by zero such as
\texttt{1/0} can
replace the default handler.  The handler receives the numerator's sign
as its argument; it may ignore it and emi the internal marker for
\texttt{NaN}, which is \texttt{I?}.
\begin{everbatim}
\def\calxDivisionByZeroAsNaN#1{I?}
\calxsetup{DivisionByZero.handler=\calxDivisionByZeroAsNaN}
\edef\result{\calxeval{1/0}}
% \result expands to NaN (which is the printed form of I?)
\end{everbatim}
Overflow independently retains its default results,
\texttt{+Infinity} and \texttt{-Infinity}.

\emph{Note that the internal markers for the non-finite values differ
from the user interface ones \texttt{NaN}, \texttt{+Infinity} and
\texttt{-Infinity}:
they are respecitevly \texttt{I?}, \texttt{I+}, \texttt{I-}. These
internal notations are \emph{only} to be used in defining handlers as above.}

\subsubsection{Testing an expression for a non-finite result}

The expandable predicate \csbxint{ifNonFinitefloatexpr} takes a
floating-point expression followed by two branches:
\begin{everbatim}
\xintifNonFinitefloatexpr{<expression>}{<non-finite>}{<finite>}
\end{everbatim}
It evaluates the expression once and selects the first branch when its
\emph{internal result} is \texttt{I?}, \texttt{I+} or \texttt{I-}.
The second branch is selected for a finite result.  For example:
\begin{everbatim*}
\xintifNonFinitefloatexpr{1/0}{YES}{NO}% YES
\xintifNonFinitefloatexpr{NaN}{YES}{NO}% YES
\xintifNonFinitefloatexpr{Infinity}{YES}{NO}% YES
\xintifNonFinitefloatexpr{sin(1)}{YES}{NO}% NO
\end{everbatim*}
This predicate examines the computed value, not the signal flags or its
printed spelling.  Evaluating an expression inside this test can itself
raise signal flags.

\subsubsection{Testing signals after a calculation}

If the calculation has already been performed, use the expandable
predicate shown below to check the flags for invalid operations,
division by zero and overflow:
\begin{everbatim}
\xintifNonFiniteSignalled{<yes>}{<no>}
\end{everbatim}
For example:
\begin{everbatim*}
\calxresetflags
\edef\result{\calxeval{1/0}}
\xintifNonFiniteSignalled
  {A non-finite condition was signalled}
  {No non-finite condition was signalled}
\end{everbatim*}

This test does not re-evaluate an expression and does not inspect
|\result|.  A handler may return a finite value while leaving a signal
flag raised; conversely, a literal \texttt{NaN} or \texttt{Infinity} need not
raise a flag.  Flags persist until cleared within their scope (e.g.\@ \LaTeX{}
environments), so use |\calxresetflags| before a calculation when only
its newly raised conditions are of interest.

\subsubsection{Signals, flags, traps and handlers}

There are eight signals:
\begin{everbatim}
Clamped          DivisionByZero  Inexact       InvalidOperation
Overflow         Rounded         Subnormal     Underflow
\end{everbatim}
Each has a flag and an independently configurable trap.  No arithmetic
trap is enabled by default.

Five additional conditions use the \texttt{InvalidOperation} flag and
trap:
\begin{everbatim}
ConversionSyntax    DivisionImpossible  DivisionUndefined
InvalidContext      ControversialOperation
\end{everbatim}
They each have individually configurable handlers.

Flags are inspected and cleared with the following commands:
\begin{everbatim}
\calxifflagraised{InvalidOperation}{<yes>}{<no>}
\calxresetflag{InvalidOperation}
\calxresetflags
\end{everbatim}
The flag test is expandable; the reset commands are assignments and are
not expandable.  Flags are local to \TeX\ groups.  Examine flags raised
inside a group before leaving it.  Reset commands obey the prevailing
\csbxint{globaldefs} setting, as do assignments by |\calxsetup|.

The default handlers for \texttt{InvalidOperation} and its subordinate
conditions return internal \texttt{I?}; the
\texttt{DivisionByZero} and \texttt{Overflow} handlers return
\texttt{I+} or \texttt{I-}, according to the sign.
The default handlers for \texttt{Clamped}, \texttt{Inexact},
\texttt{Rounded}, \texttt{Subnormal} and \texttt{Underflow}
leave the computed result unchanged.

Traps and handlers are configured with |\calxsetup|:
\begin{everbatim}
\calxsetup{InvalidOperation.trap=true}
\calxsetup{Overflow.trap=false}
\calxsetup{ControversialOperation.handler=\myHandler}
\end{everbatim}
The value of a \texttt{.handler} key is a single control sequence
denoting an expandable one-argument handler.  An unknown setup key or
invalid trap value is a configuration error and is reported by \TeX,
regardless of the arithmetic-trap settings.



