\subsubsection{\csh{xintunassignvar}}
\label{xintunassignvar}

\csa{xintunassignvar}\marg{variable} will make the \meta{variable} un-assigned.
For example in the previous section we used
\begin{everbatim}
\xintdeffloatvar e := ...some value...;
\end{everbatim}
To undo one either waits for the current scope (e.g.\@ a \LaTeX{}
environment) to expire or the impatient does:
\begin{everbatim*}
\xintunassignvar{e}
\end{everbatim*}

In this special case\IMPORTANT{} of using \csa{xintunassignvar} with a
single \meta{letter} the effect is actually to let 
the \meta{letter} recover the meaning of a dummy variable (i.e.\@ it is
the same as using \csbxint{newdummy} documented in the next section):
\begin{everbatim*}
% overwriting a dummy letter
\xintdefvar i := 3;%
\xinteval{i^3} is as expected but |\xinteval{add(i,i=1..10)}| computes 
\xinteval{add(i,i=1..10)} because "i" has the fixed value 3.\newline
\xintunassignvar{i}% back to normal
After |\xintunassignvar{i}|
|\xinteval{add(i,i=..10)}| is evluated with "i" acting as a dummy variable
and thus outputs \xinteval{add(i,i=1..10)}.\par
\end{everbatim*}

Under \csbxint{globaldefstrue} regime the effect of \csa{xintunassignvar} is
of global scope.

\subsubsection{\csh{xintnewdummy}}
\label{xintnewdummy}


Any catcode 11 character can serve as a dummy variable, via this declaration:
\begin{everbatim}
\xintnewdummy{<letter>}% the <letter> must be of catcode letter!
\end{everbatim}
For example with Lua\TeX\ or Xe\TeX\ the following works:
\begin{everbatim}
% Requires a Unicode engine
\xintnewdummy{ξ}
\xinteval{add(ξ, ξ=1..10)}
\end{everbatim}

Starting with |1.4|, it is allowed to use \csa{xintnewdummy} to define
``dummy variables'' having names with more than one letter.  They can
then be used as expected:
\begin{everbatim}
% Requires a Unicode engine
\xintnewdummy{ατλν}
\xintdeffunc test(ατλν) = sqr(1 + ατλν);
\xinteval{seq(test(ατλν), ατλν = 0..10)}
\end{everbatim}

Remark regarding Op\TeX: it is a format using Lua\TeX, but non ascii
letters have catcode ``other''.  So for the above example to compile
with |optex|, the catcodes need to be set to ``letter'' beforehand:
\begin{everbatim}
% Requires a Unicode engine
% next line is required if with OpTeX (only):
\xintFor* #1 in {ατλν}\do{\catcode`#1=11 }
\xintnewdummy{ατλν}
\xintdeffunc test(ατλν) = sqr(1 + ατλν);
\xinteval{seq(test(ατλν), ατλν = 0..10)}
\end{everbatim}

Under \csbxint{globaldefstrue} regime the effect of \csa{xintnewdummy} is
of global scope.

\subsubsection{\csh{xintensuredummy}, \csh{xintrestorevariable}}
\label{xintensuredummy}
\label{xintrestorevariable}

Use
\begin{everbatim}
\xintensuredummy{<character>}
...
... code using the (catcode 11) character as a dummy variable
...
\xintrestorevariable{<character>}
\end{everbatim}
if other parts need the letter as an assigned variable name. For example
\calxtrigname being written at high level needs a few genuine dummy variables,
and it uses \csbxint{ensuredummy} to be certain everything is ok.

\subsection{User defined functions}
\label{ssec:userfunctions}
\def\tocstylesubsubsectionHOOK#1#2#3{}
\etocsetnexttocdepth{subsubsection}
\localtableofcontents
