\subsection{Introduction}

This library (not to be loaded directly by user)
 provides trigonometric functions for use within
\csbxint{floateval} (and \csbxint{eval}), up to a maximal accuracy of
\dtt{100} floating point digits\NewWith{2.0a1}.

This first version supporting trigonoometry was release |1.3e|
(|2019/04/05|) of \xintexprname.  It was completely rewrittent at
|2.0a1|.  It now supports precisions up to \dtt{100}, is faster and more
accurate.  It defines no user level macro.

% At |1.4e| (|2021/05/05|) the accuracy of the trailing digits was significantly
% increased.%
% %
% \footnote{Prior to 2021, the high-level user interface was used to
%   define the functions and intermediate operations were done without
%   guard digits. As a result the last two digits were most of the time
%   off, at least the last one.}

% Release |1.4p| adds the aliases \func{arcsin}, \func{arccos} and \func{arctg},
% and the reciprocal functions \func{acsc} and \func{asec} with aliases
% \func{arccsc} and \func{arcsec}.

At \csbxint{theDigits} set to \dtt{8} (or less), the computations are
targeting a slightly degraded accuracy (roughly at 8 digits, only about
95\% of values are correctly rounded, compared to 99.5\% for |P>8|.  In
both cases the error is never more tha 1ulp.  Actually the |2.0a1| implementation
appears to be excessively precise so the indicated percentage of correct rounding
is actually very conservative.

