\funcdesc{log1p} computes |log(1+x)|

\funcdesc{exp} exponential function

\funcdesc{expm1} computes |e^x-1|

\funcdesc[x, y]{pow} computes $x^y$ via the formula |pow(y*log(x))|.
The exponent |y| is not rounded to the target floating point precision
beforehand, but to this target precision plus 14.  It does not have to
be, if integer, at most the \TeX{} bound for integers (which is
\dtt{\xintiieval{"7FFFFFFF}}). But the output must have a decimal
exponent obeying this bound (with some safety margin)
\begin{everbatim*}
\calxeval{3^3350214776, 2^5309964787}

\calxeval{3^3350214776/2^5309964787}
\end{everbatim*}

Inside the \csbxint{eval} evaluator, and if the exponent is integer,  and
if it is anticipated that
the output will not exceed (or will only slightly exceed) \dtt{10000} digits
(separately for numerator and denominator), then the power is computed \emph{exactly}.
To compute exactly higher powers than |2^9999| or |9^9999| or |99^5000| or
|999^3333|, etc..., use \csbxint{iieval}.  See \csbxint{iiPow} for related
comments if you don't want to melt your CPU.


