\section{Macros of the \xintbinhexname package}
\RaisedLabel{sec:binhex}

\localtableofcontents

\subsection{Introduction}

This package provides expandable conversions of (big) integers between
the hexadecimal, decimal, octal (since |1.4n|) and binary bases.

First version of this package was in the |1.08| (|2013/06/07|) release
of \xintname. Its routines remained unmodified until their complete
rewrite at release |1.2m| (|2017/07/31|). Macros became faster, but the
inputs got limited to a few thousand digits, whereas the |1.08| versions
could handle (slowly...) tens of thousands of digits.  At |1.4n| some
internals were refactored to use the \cs{expanded} primitive (which was
not available in 2017).\NewWith{1.4n}  The maximal sizes got increased, see
\autoref{ssec:binhexsizes}.  More significant probably, the octal radix
was added to the ones covered.

|1.4p| added \csbxint{LowerHex} and \csbxint{UpperHex}.\NewWith{1.4p}


For each provided conversion macro, its argument is first \fexpan
ded. This expansion is supposed to give a sequence of digits, with
perhaps a (unique) leading minus sign, which gets prepended to output
(note that \csbxint{DecToHex}|{-0}| thus expands to \dtt{\xintDecToHex{-0}}).

Let's insist that inputs can not start (after expansion) with a |0b|,
|0o|, |0x|, |#x|, |"|, |'|, or similar prefix notation: they must
consist only of digits as fitting to the binary, octal, decimal, or
hexadecimal radix.  Situation is different if using \xintexprname-essions,
see \autoref{ssec:exprbinhex} next.

Low-level unrecoverable errors will occur if for
example an octal input contains the decimal digit |8|.

Hexadecimal digits in input must be uppercased. Category codes for
them may be indifferently \emph{letter} or \emph{other}. In output they
are of category \emph{letter} (and uppercased).

Leading zeroes in the input are allowed, and depending on the macro may
show up or not in the output.  Note in particular:
\begin{itemize}[noitemsep]
\item Inputs with no leading zeros give outputs with no leading zeros.

\item All rules have (deliberate) exceptions, check the docs of
  \csbxint{CHexToBin} and
  \csbxint{COctToBin} which are variants of \csbxint{HexToBin} and
  \csbxint{OctToBin}.

\item Outputs (if non vanishing) from \csbxint{DecToHex} or \csbxint{DecToOct}
  have no leading zeros whether or not the inputs had some.

\item \csbxint{BinToHex} and \csbxint{BinToOct} always use the minimal number of
  hexadecimal resp.\@ octal digits as needed to represent the original binary digits,
  inclusive of their leading zeroes. For example
  \csbxint{BinToHex}|{0000001}| outputs \dtt{\xintBinToHex{0000001}} and
  \csbxint{BinToOct}|{0000001}| outputs \dtt{\xintBinToOct{0000001}}.
\end{itemize}


\subsection{\xintexprname-essions}
\label{ssec:exprbinhex}

Inside \xintexprname-essions,\NewWith{1.4n} hexadecimal can be input
using either |"| or |0x| prefixes, octal using either |'| or |0o|, and
binary using |0b|.  Prior to |1.4n| only |"| was implemented and it was
needed to load \xintbinhexname additionally to \xintexprname.  This is
now done automatically.

Hexadecimal letters must be uppercased.  In both of \csbxint{eval} and
\csbxint{floateval} a ``fractional part'' after the full stop as
separator is allowed for all three bases.  Here is an example using the
three non-decimal bases:
\begin{everbatim*}
\xinteval{subsm({x, y, x==y}, x="FF.FF * '777.777 * 0b11111.11111;
                              y=(16^2-16^-2)(8^3-8^-3)(2^5-2^-5);)}
\end{everbatim*}

The
|p| postfix notation from some programming languages, which stands for
an extra power of two, is however not implemented so far.

With \csbxint{iieval}\NewWith{1.4n}, which handles only integers, there
is an optional parameter |[h]|, |[o]|, or |[b]| for automatic conversion
of the output (this works also with comma separated inputs and even
nested bracketed inputs).  Here is an example illustrating the new
more condensed syntax:
\begin{everbatim*}
\xintiieval[b]{0b1011100101001110 * 0b111100011}
\end{everbatim*}

Compare with how one would have had to input it prior to |1.4n|:
\begin{everbatim*}
\xintDecToBin{\xintiieval{\xintBinToDec{1011100101001110}*\xintBinToDec{111100011}}}
\end{everbatim*}

Note that if while parsing an octal number the digits |8| or |9| is
encountered, this does not trigger un error but terminates the octal
input fetched so far and inserts a tacit multiplication.  Same for
binary input if encountering a digit other than |0| or |1|.


