\subsection{\csh{xintLowerHex}}\label{xintLowerHex}

The output\etype{f} is the lowercase version of the input (which can be
with mixed case).  An empty input is allowed.\NewWith{1.4p}  Letters on input can be of
catcode \emph{letter} or \emph{other}. Letters in output are of catcode
\emph{letter}.
\begin{everbatim*}
\xintLowerHex{11A9397C66949A97051F7D0A817914E3E0B17C41B11C48BAEF2B5760BB38D272F}\newline
\xintLowerHex{\xintDecToHex{12379813738877118345}}
\end{everbatim*}

As it \fexpan ds its argument, you can as shown above use it to wrap usage
of one of the package macros converting to hexadecimal.

\subsection{\csh{xintUpperHex}}\label{xintUpperHex}

The output\etype{f} is the uppercase version of the input (which can be
with mixed case).  An empty input is allowed.\NewWith{1.4p} Letters on input can be of
catcode \emph{letter} or \emph{other}. Letters in output are of catcode
\emph{letter}.
\begin{everbatim*}
\xintUpperHex{11a9397c66949a97051f7d0a817914e3e0b17c41b11c48baef2b5760bb38d272f}\newline
\xintHexToDec{\xintUpperHex{abcdef0123456789}}
\end{everbatim*}

As shown above, use it to wrap the argument of \csbxint{HexToDec} if for
example some lowercase, or mixed case, hexadecimal input needs to get
converted to decimal.

\subsection{Maximal sizes of inputs}\label{ssec:binhexsizes}

\autoref{tab:binhexsizes} recapitulates the maximal allowed sizes, as
found out with the \TeX{} installation of the author.  The tests are done
putting the macro inside an \cs{edef} and compiling with the |etex| binary.

The value in the second column is the maximal |N| such that the macro
does not raise an error on an input with |N| digits (if nested in
another macro, the maximal input size may become lower than stated).
The third column gives the corresponding maximal size of the output.

These maximal sizes depend on \TeX\ parameters such as input stack size,
expansion depth, and parameter stack size.  The fourth column gives the
\TeX\ parameter cited in the error message when trying with |N+1|
digits.  When the limiting parameter is the main memory size, the upper
limits depend on external factors such as how many macros are loaded in
\TeX{} memory (for example they would be lower in a \LaTeX{} document or
if \xintexprname is loaded), so they are here given simply as
indications.  They are so large anyhow that basically in practice, this
means no real limitation.

Regarding the conversions to and from decimal radix, they allow a more
limited range, but are still able to handle inputs with ten thousand
digits, one can thus consider in practice that their size limitations
are also only of theoretical interest.

