%)
% Multiplication. \CALX_nmul{X}{Y}. The kernels are used as they are, from
% the renamed copy of xintcore (they are self contained, the addition they
% use is the unmodified \CALX_add_a): \CALX_mul_loop, \CALX_smallmul,
% \CALX_verysmallmul. Only the dispatch (xintcore's \XINT_mul_checklengths,
% \XINT_mul_smallbyfirst/second) is rewritten, with the stored block counts,
% and the output.
%
% Contracts (from the code, xintcore l.1013-1097 code-only):
%- \the\numexpr\CALX_mul_loop 100000000!1;!\W <X>1;!\W <Y>1;! computes
% X*Y, iterating over the blocks of Y (the initial 100000000!1;! is the
% zero accumulator); output: product blocks LS-first then 1;!, then the
% tokens which followed Y's 1;!;
%- \the\numexpr\CALX_smallmul 1<y>!<X>1;! for a single block y, and
% \the\numexpr\CALX_verysmallmul 0\xint:<y>!<X>1;! for y<22 (xintcore's
% threshold): same output form.
%
% These kernels never produce a zero top block (the final carry block is
% dropped when zero, \XINT_smallmul_f, \XINT_verysmallmul_f). A product
% of n and m blocks has n+m-1 or n+m blocks: \CALX_nmulo<sign> receives
% n+m-1 (computed before the kernel), skips that many blocks
