%\begin{lverb}
% Syntax (use with \romannumeral0; P a \numexpr expression; X f-expanded
% F, I or raw value):
%(
%  \CALX_fsqr{P}{X}   -> <space>Z    Z = round_P(round_P(X)^2)
%:  \CALX_fsqru{P}{X}  -> <space>Z    (X finite F, at most P digits)
%)
% \CALX_fsqr{P}{X} (use with \romannumeral0, X f-expanded):
% round_P(round_P(X)^2), round half away from zero, as xint's
% \XINTinFloatSqr[P]; the same result as \CALX_fmul{P}{X}{X}, with the
% operand read once and kernels which compute each cross product once
% (x_i x_j + x_j x_i written as 2 x_i x_j).
%
% Special values: I? gives I?, I+ and I- give I+. Zero gives F+0.0.
%
% P <= 16 and a mantissa of at most 24 digits: one extraction as in
% \CALX_fmul (\CALX_fm_xvi, \CALX_fm_ex), then \CALX_fs_k (the square of
% the 16 digit A, seven columns, ten products) and \CALX_fm_r. Otherwise
% \CALX_fs_big: \CALX_fround, then by P: \CALX_fs_q (P <= 20),
% \CALX_fs_v (P <= 24), \CALX_fs_h (P <= 34), \CALX_fs_g (generic, also
% the fallback of \CALX_fs_v and \CALX_fs_h). They read the operand as
% \CALX_fm_q, \CALX_fm_v, \CALX_fm_h read X, with exponents
%(
%:   E = 16e + 2D - 40,   E = 16e + 2D - 48,   E = 16e + 2D - 60,
%)
% and finish as these. With x_i the groups of four digits, the column
% c_k of A^2 is
%(
%:   c_k = 2 (sum of x_i x_(k-i), i < k - i) + x_(k/2)^2,
%)
% the square term only for k even: its value is at most that of the
% column of a product (same number of terms), so the carry scheme of
% \CALX_fm_s is unchanged; the truncation bounds of \CALX_fm_v and
% \CALX_fm_h hold as they are (they only use the sizes of the operands).
% \CALX_fs_q$empty: 15 products instead of 25, one kernel macro of five
% arguments; \CALX_fs_v$empty: 15 instead of 26, one macro of six arguments;
% \CALX_fs_h$empty: two kernels instead of three (AH AL once, \CALX_fs_ra for
